#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18aa5b7-c992-5b32-a357-5e724ba96bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9+\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c18aa5b7-c992-5b32-a357-5e724ba96bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9+\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293782} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4170770-80a0-5c01-a8a3-394cfa36e747" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f14565cc-b995-5571-b40e-c5bb6b6d3c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-316261" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/d36f2859865da58d226a738d8338f726df950db75b7421b604bb692c9049d54a.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-2,1].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0bf7010-3f64-5b22-8fc4-82fc0116c4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293785} {:answer/id #uuid "eabd0215-ed94-5435-bebd-84d0f8f97cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0bf7010-3f64-5b22-8fc4-82fc0116c4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293785} :answer-field/id #uuid "603a54c5-8911-5f08-b53b-a8d967ee08be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "550da44a-dc56-593e-bbdf-dd12d1acd2aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-338507" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/8eb7c4fe147028c6dc31aad2427a33bb713553e610101b1fd263bda30f5c49d0.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-4],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-4].$   The corresponding region is a triangle below the $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$   The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0).$   The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,5].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)(5) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2}+6+10 \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91204a75-1a83-5274-8195-9162d1e95fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14-2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91204a75-1a83-5274-8195-9162d1e95fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14-2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293789} :answer-field/id #uuid "630dbbfe-452a-5391-8ba4-f98903b06972" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3ea9770-cd37-54c4-9097-58e7386e8964" :question/math-academy-id "q-353136" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/f7eebd794b43da16658750db0df3a4d4c4b894ddb80f84795ea483aa4d579f56.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in [0,4),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (4,6].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a lower semi-circle of radius $2$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (4,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-2\\pi +8+6 \\\\  & =14-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "537bdde3-b02a-5a7e-91d5-dc508c1c30b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293792} {:answer/id #uuid "8afd4198-41eb-5684-af29-1523fceb95fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293793} {:answer/id #uuid "e798e2f6-3045-5934-9b47-f421e52efb21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293794} {:answer/id #uuid "ee26fc1e-31f0-56bc-8bfa-cc970beb02d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293795} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6c630f-a465-58dc-b7f4-eca8d3851383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e798e2f6-3045-5934-9b47-f421e52efb21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293794} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c123162-227f-5a83-9cf7-7153866e7bca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df468f4e-2d83-51ff-a604-abd18e09aab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-49206" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/22472e04f7bd26076da43a2ad17a192726ad19b5bd3885ce066c9523cad4f870.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of three parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-1,1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle. Therefore, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.5.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+3)(2) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-1.5+5+3 \\\\  & =6.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdbdddce-6bb9-5d6f-bd08-be7557a8f000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293799} {:answer/id #uuid "4909127d-d3ef-50aa-9e10-a02ce28169eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293800} {:answer/id #uuid "97255af8-a483-51c4-80e3-0129d7413bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293801} {:answer/id #uuid "77f0a833-75df-5102-8814-f868d63b9f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293802} {:answer/id #uuid "cccd6aab-30ef-5d1e-b5c2-16056043112d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cccd6aab-30ef-5d1e-b5c2-16056043112d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293803} :answer-field/id #uuid "2014b57f-2aff-53dd-89c9-a9eda5d5e635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f31c4e7-003d-5a6c-9983-dff54f594d65" :question/math-academy-id "q-86174" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/3e0eca1a04396c0c86bbf5c3e5a9b3ff7071eacfe3d8c4281549e70cda809e8c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a right triange, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+(4-\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
